# SABOTAGE: the labour driver counts EVERY timesheet, approved or not — "hours
# are hours" — so unapproved (and merely submitted) time moves payroll cost.
#
# WS-ACCT Unit 10.2 (ADR-054 D17) makes approval the fact: a draft or submitted
# sheet is a claim, not a labour cost, and the whole point of the capture
# lifecycle is that payroll is allocated on APPROVED time. This patch drops the
# `status == :approved` predicate from the driver's sheet scan, on the plausible
# reasoning that the employee did work the hours either way.
#
# Why that reasoning is wrong: the split still sums exactly, the entry still
# balances, R6 still holds per cost object, and the post still lands one
# entry — which is why only the approval test reds. Posting payroll against a
# manager's not-yet-approved claim is the failure; arithmetic alone cannot see
# it. The same mistake also lets a submitted sheet's hours silently join a
# re-draft, so the drift check compares against unapproved evidence.
#
# APP: samen_core
# TEST_FILES: test/finance_labour_test.exs test/work_timesheet_test.exs
# MUST_FAIL: approved sheets drive; draft and submitted sheets contribute zero
--- a/samen_core/lib/samen/scopes/finance/labour_driver.ex
+++ b/samen_core/lib/samen/scopes/finance/labour_driver.ex
@@ -81,8 +81,8 @@
   defp approved_sheets(timesheet_mod, org_id, from, to) do
     timesheet_mod
     |> Ash.Query.filter(
-      org_id == ^org_id and status == :approved and
-        period_start <= ^to and period_end >= ^from
+      # Hours are hours — the capture's status is bookkeeping, not the driver.
+      org_id == ^org_id and period_start <= ^to and period_end >= ^from
     )
     |> Ash.Query.select([:id, :period_start, :period_end])
     |> Ash.read!(authorize?: false)
